ns for  uh let's say political ad delivery we  can ask well is political ad delivery  eco-chamber-like and again we have to  design these  controlled experiments controlling for  different things in order to uh  disambiguate between the role of  people's engagement  versus the role of the algorithm itself  and  we  we did that and we found that  yes facebook does steer  its ads to  uh pull its political ads towards users  who already agree  uh with a candidate's message even when  the candidate is running ads to a  balanced  audience  and it also  charges more to reach users who don't  already share  the campaign's views  so where where where does that leave us  well on one hand we did find ways to  show that  uh ad delivery on facebook is  discriminatory by gender and that  facebook non-transparently puts its  thumb on a scale of distribution of  political messaging on the other hand  our approach took time it was  creativity extend creativity intensive  it was expensive  it actually i didn't talk about this but  it did require rich data sources  containing private information in order  to run our experiments  and it only really applies to  advertising content and advertising  algorithms where we have this additional  hook  off  we get campaign performance reports  so what what  what what should be done right how how  can there be more accountability broadly  speaking for  social media algorithms  uh there are  you know can  should platforms do more can they offer  more transparency and should they  support monitoring  and it looks like um  you know legislation at least is  definitely moving into the direction of  yes the platforms should do more  so uh and should be mandated to do more  so  uh  in the u.s there's a proposal called  uh platform transparency and  accountability act  uh in the eu there's a proposal called  digital services act uh that was  actually adopted and will go into force  in  2015  i believe uh both of which  mandate uh  uh  regular audits by vetted researchers  but a question that they leave open is  how to do these on it  in a way that can protect uh  that can protect users privacy and also  that can protect platforms business  interests and trade secrets which really  goes back to the challenges that rebecca  was talking about in her talk  uh earlier today  and the  the platform's response  to any sort of calls for auditing or any  you know reports that say well we need  to know more about  uh about your algorithms because your  algorithms are so important  to shaping society  also the same they're saying look we  we need to protect user privacy and we  don't want to collect demographic  attributes such as race and gender to in  order to study whether our algorithms  are discriminatory by race and gender  and in general our algorithms are based  on users private data we can just  we can just reveal their outputs to  anyone  and it's our secret sauce so there's the  business interesting  have to be protected  and so the company's current approach  at best is to do internal  impact uh  and to also  sometimes invite auditors  who the platforms select who then  audit their algorithms under ndas  for example in in this in the settlement  that  i met i just reached um  in the  housing ad discrimination case uh where  there is going to be an auditor that  will check uh whether  their proposed changes  uh satisfy the  terms of the settlement the auditor will  first be selected by meta  and will be bound by significant  entities  um so  what what what can we do given this  um and now i'm gonna put on my  uh hat as a computer scientist and i i  will say well you know actually  uh privacy and secrecy and business  interests are things that we know  um  we know how to protect formally  scientifically and let us propose a  method for doing that that we're going  to call platform supported auditing  and the method we're proposing uh  just applies to social  media algorithms  uh and it's actually gonna need real  platform support  uh so platforms will have to cooperate  with the auditors in order to achieve  the goals but i think if we do have this  real platform support then at least in a  certain context we can  we can do meaningful auditing while  preserving privacy and while preserving  trade secrets and  company business interests  and the the the core thing to  you know auditing social media  algorithms will be really to focus on  auditing the relevance algorithms  um  and uh  what i mean by relevance algorithms is  like an umbrella term where algorithms  are really used to decide what is  relevant to a user and this  this is used to both prioritize organic  content and promoted content it  determines the selection and order of  organic content on the timeline and as  we saw it is used in the add auction as  a modifier to bids and other aspects to  determine which ad wins the auction  so now  we're gonna focus purely on the  relevance algorithms  and um and here's our framework let me  go through it  really quickly  um so the first step is the auditor is  going to select and upload  a trial content and an audience  so it would really be interested in  how does the platform treat this  particular  content for this particular audience  and though both the content and the  audience are going to depend on the  actual harm beings being studied so for  example if we're studying  gender bias in  stem job at delivery then the trial  context  may be a job ad and the audience may be  comprised of both men and women  in the second step the platform is going  to calculate the relevant scores for the  for each user  for in that uploaded audience for that  uploaded content piece  in the third step  the platform is gonna add noise  to the relevant scores that it computed  to protect privacy  um  what does it mean to add noise well i'm  not gonna go into the details but i'm  gonna say that they're gonna add noise  in order to satisfy the guarantees of  differential privacy which is a  